    descriptionEn: "Blues bands emerged in the Mississippi Delta between 1920-1940, crystallizing the evolution from rural acoustic blues to urban electrification. 'Blues' derives from 'blue devils,' an English expression for deep melancholy. This genre fuses plantation work songs, African-American spirituals, and European ballads, carrying memories of slavery and segregation. The 12-bar AAB structure remains fundamental. Culturally, blues bands transcend entertainment to become vehicles of social emancipation, giving voice to the oppressed. B.B. King's vibrato mastery, Muddy Waters' electric Delta sound, and Buddy Guy's explosive energy transform pain into universal art. This musical language influences rock, jazz, and soul globally. From Chicago's South Side clubs to international stages, blues bands forge an authentic emotional connection, making personal struggle a collective cathartic experience that resonates across cultural boundaries.",
