    descriptionEn: "Chicha emerged in 1960s-70s Lima's sprawling pueblos jóvenes, a revolutionary fusion of Colombian cumbia and Andean indigenous music. Named after 'chicha,' the sacred fermented corn beverage, it embodies urban mestizo identity. Born from massive rural-to-urban migration, chicha gave voice to marginalized Andean migrants excluded by Lima's creole elite. Recorded in makeshift studios with minimal resources, chicha developed its signature lo-fi aesthetic through necessity. The genre transcended music, becoming cultural expression for an emerging social class that created a hybrid identity reconciling Andean tradition with urban modernity. This sonic rebellion challenged Peru's rigid social hierarchies, establishing chicha as authentic voice of the marginalized while profoundly influencing contemporary Latin American music, inspiring international revival movements decades later.",
