    descriptionEn: "Classic garage rock crystallized between 1963-1967, emerging simultaneously from American suburbs and British working-class neighborhoods during the post-war cultural upheaval. This raw sound fused early rock 'n' roll with R&B intensity and folk rock's rebellious spirit.\n\nFarfisa and Vox Continental organs added psychedelic textures.\n\nThree-chord structures dominated, with minimal production emphasizing live energy over technical precision. Reverb-drenched vocals and primitive recording techniques created the genre's signature lo-fi aesthetic.\n\nCulturally, garage rock embodied teenage rebellion against establishment values, predating punk's anti-authoritarian stance by a decade. It democratized rock music, proving that passion trumped technical virtuosity. The genre's DIY ethos influenced countless subsequent movements, from proto-punk to indie rock, establishing the template for underground music culture and inspiring generations of musicians to embrace raw authenticity over commercial sophistication.",
