    descriptionEn: "Peruvian folklore represents a vibrant musical heritage born from the convergence of pre-Hispanic traditions, Spanish colonization, and African influences since the 16th century. This encompassing term designates the ensemble of popular musical expressions transmitted orally across Peru's three geographical regions: costa (coast), sierra (highlands), and selva (Amazon jungle). The musical scales blend pentatonic pre-Columbian modes with European diatonic systems, creating distinctive harmonic progressions. Culturally, this music articulates identity resistance and social cohesion, serving as vehicle for historical narratives, agricultural cycles, and syncretic religious celebrations. Its contemporary influence radiates through Nueva Canción movement and inspires international artists like Dylan and Mitchell, who discovered in these ancient melodies a profound authenticity missing from Western folk traditions.",
