    descriptionEn: "Instrumental rock emerged in the 1960s-1970s, primarily in Britain and the United States, as a natural extension of psychedelic and progressive rock movements. The term literally describes rock compositions devoid of vocal parts, prioritizing pure instrumental expression. Born from American electric blues influence and California's surf rock instrumental tradition, the genre finds its roots in British bands' studio experimentations. The genre revolutionized musical virtuosity perception, transforming guitarists into genuine sonic storytellers. Instrumental rock profoundly influenced progressive metal development and film scoring, establishing the paradigm of emotional expression through instruments alone, without textual mediation. This approach created a universal musical language transcending linguistic barriers, where technical mastery serves narrative purpose, making each performance a wordless dialogue between musician and audience through pure sonic communication.",
