    name: "nuevo flamenco",
    descriptionFr: "Le nuevo flamenco émerge dans les années 1980 en Espagne, principalement en Andalousie, avant de conquérir la scène musicale internationale. Né de la volonté d'artistes comme Paco de Lucía et plus tard Jesse Cook d'explorer de nouveaux territoires sonores, ce genre intègre des éléments jazz, world music, pop et électroniques au patrimoine flamenco ancestral. Les instruments emblématiques incluent la guitare flamenca Conde Hermanos ou Ramírez, souvent amplifiée, accompagnée de cajón péruvien, violons électriques, synthétiseurs et parfois de la batterie. Cette innovation musicale symbolise l'ouverture du flamenco au monde globalisé, créant des ponts interculturels tout en préservant son essence passionnelle. Le nuevo flamenco démocratise cet art séculaire, le rendant accessible à un public international sans en trahir l'âme andalouse.",
    descriptionEn: "Nuevo flamenco emerged in the 1980s in Spain, primarily in Andalusia, before conquering international music scenes. This revolutionary fusion derives its name from the alliance between traditional flamenco ('nuevo' meaning 'new' in Spanish) and various contemporary influences. Born from artists like Paco de Lucía and later Jesse Cook seeking new sonic territories, this genre integrates jazz, world music, pop, and electronic elements into ancestral flamenco heritage. Emblematic instruments include Conde Hermanos or Ramírez flamenco guitars, often amplified, accompanied by Peruvian cajón, electric violins, synthesizers, and sometimes drums. This musical innovation symbolizes flamenco's opening to the globalized world, creating intercultural bridges while preserving its passionate essence. Nuevo flamenco democratizes this secular art form, making it accessible to international audiences without betraying its Andalusian soul. The genre's cultural significance lies in its ability to modernize tradition, creating a universal language that speaks to both purists and newcomers, establishing flamenco as a living, evolving art form rather than a museum piece.",
