    name: "roda de samba",
    descriptionFr: "La roda de samba émerge dans les favelas de Rio de Janeiro durant les années 1920-1930, cristallisant l'essence communautaire du samba traditionnel.\n\nCe genre fusionne les traditions afro-brésiliennes, le choro carioca et les influences portugaises. L'instrumentation privilégie le cavaquinho (souvent Rozini ou Del Vecchio), la guitare classique 7 cordes, le pandeiro Gope, le tantã, le reco-reco et le surdo. Les percussions Contemporânea dominent les formations professionnelles.\n\nLe violão de 7 cordas développe des baixarias (lignes de basse mélodiques) complexes, tandis que le cavaquinho exécute des centros rythmiques syncopés.\n\nCulturellement, la roda de samba transcende le simple divertissement pour devenir un espace de résistance culturelle afro-brésilienne. Reconnue patrimoine immatériel par l'UNESCO en 2005, elle perpétue la tradition orale, favorise l'improvisation collective et maintient vivante la mémoire des compositeurs populaires du Brésil urbain.",
    descriptionEn: "Roda de samba emerged in Rio de Janeiro's favelas during the early 20th century, particularly in neighborhoods like Estácio and Mangueira around the 1920s-1930s.\n\nThis genre fused African polyrhythmic traditions brought by enslaved peoples with Portuguese folk melodies and indigenous Brazilian elements. Core instrumentation includes cavaquinho (small four-string guitar), pandeiro (Brazilian tambourine), surdo drums, cuíca friction drums, and seven-string classical guitars. Modern rodas often feature Rozini or Giannini cavaquinhos and Contemporânea percussion.\n\nProduction remains deliberately raw and acoustic, preserving the intimate, spontaneous essence.\n\nCulturally, roda de samba represents resistance and community building within marginalized Afro-Brazilian populations. These gatherings fostered cultural preservation, social solidarity, and musical innovation, eventually influencing bossa nova and contemporary MPB while maintaining its grassroots authenticity as UNESCO-recognized cultural heritage.",
