    descriptionFr: "Le scottish indie émerge au début des années 1980 à Glasgow et Édimbourg, fusionnant l'indie rock britannique avec l'héritage folk écossais.\n\nLa production emphasise reverbs naturelles et delays analogiques, créant cette atmosphère brumeuse caractéristique des landes écossaises.\n\nCulturellement, le scottish indie accompagne la renaissance identitaire écossaise post-dévolution, véhiculant un sentiment de fierté régionale tout en s'ouvrant internationalement. Il influence durablement la scène indie européenne, établissant Glasgow comme capitale alternative majeure et inspirant une génération d'artistes revendiquant leurs racines locales face à la globalisation musicale anglo-américaine.",
    descriptionEn: "Scottish indie emerged in the mid-1980s across Glasgow, Edinburgh, and Dundee, crystallizing Scotland's distinctive approach to independent rock music.\n\nMusically, Scottish indie fused traditional Celtic folk melodies with post-punk's angular rhythms and jangly guitar pop, incorporating elements from C86, new wave, and alternative rock.\n\nProduction styles vary from lo-fi bedroom recordings to polished studio work, often incorporating reverb-heavy vocals and layered harmonies. Drum machines and analog synthesizers frequently supplement traditional rock instrumentation.\n\nCulturally, Scottish indie represented working-class artistic expression during Thatcher-era economic decline, fostering DIY ethics and community-centered music scenes. Glasgow's independent venues like King Tut's Wah Wah Hut became crucial incubators. The genre's legacy includes influencing Britpop and nurturing internationally successful acts, while maintaining distinctly Scottish lyrical themes addressing urban alienation, social commentary, and regional identity through melodic accessibility and intelligent songcraft.",
